## Acceptance clarification: include the real developer journey and artifact truth

v0.5.4 must prove not only abstract portability but also the complete frozen developer experience.

Acceptance must include:

- pristine repository -> guided `baha app init` -> `baha up`;
- deterministic/non-interactive CI path as a separate scenario;
- repository Compose/source remains read-only;
- capability-oriented intent and provider resolution are visibly distinct;
- default provider realization (e.g. PostgreSQL for SQL) is observable without leaking into portable intent;
- stale runtime image detection;
- changed vs unchanged repository `build:` convergence;
- Docker and Podman semantic parity;
- human CLI, verbose diagnostics, JSON and MCP agree on the same desired/realized/verified truth;
- update path reports application-facing changes, provider/runtime version changes and recovery implications;
- no false READY from stale/incompatible artifacts.

This acceptance should reuse the concrete happy-path/demo work from #372 and the correctness/UX work from #367–#379 rather than inventing a separate v0.5 developer workflow.

## Portability acceptance must prove #390

#300 should explicitly prove the #390 standards-first boundary:
- same service intent with different conforming providers;
- Service Binding 1.1 names remain stable across providers;
- protocol compatibility requirements do not become product selection;
- Provider Catalog metadata does not leak into portable application intent;
- standard-backed services use the adopted standards;
- BaseHarbor extensions are isolated/versioned;
- vector remains minimal where no neutral standard exists.

## Standards-first portability acceptance

Include #391 in final portability acceptance.

Acceptance must prove:
- portable service schemas contain no provider product fields;
- Service Binding 1.1 well-known names are used for generic connection outputs;
- legacy environment-variable projections do not become the canonical portable binding model;
- cache providers can advertise RESP compatibility without making Redis the service identity;
- object-storage providers can advertise S3 compatibility without making AWS S3 the service identity;
- observability uses OpenTelemetry/OTLP rather than a parallel Baha telemetry wire format;
- identity uses OIDC/OAuth semantics rather than proprietary login/token semantics;
- messaging design uses AsyncAPI/CloudEvents where applicable;
- vector remains a deliberately small BaseHarbor extension because no accepted neutral service standard exists.
